l tell  you a bit of a bit more about it but it  it really helped us manage cost it's  really hard to manage costs in a Colo  I'm for a SAS company it really helped  us make our platform more scalable I'll  show you some  about that and more reliable which  really can't say enough about that in  terms of just the quality of life of our  engineers and just the customer  satisfaction that we now enjoy and also  now that we're on the other side of it  we no longer are doing crazy things like  balancing Cassandra rings and HBase  clusters and tuning Kafka and so our  engineers are able now to innovate much  further up the stack which is which is  great  so just briefly a little bit about urban  airship  urban airship is a customer engagement  platform so we focused primarily on  things like retention and growth for our  customers we helped a little bit on the  acquisition side but mostly focused on  those two things we started you may know  us we were the original third-party push  notification company but we've expanded  dramatically and create many different  types of experiences these days we  expanded into you know various in app  experiences we work with Google and  Apple on things like Google pay so  mobile wallets yeah we power a lot of  your boarding passes probably if you  flew some of the Airlines that we  support we do things on the web email  SMS some other channels as well and we  operate at a pretty great scale ad Tech  is the only thing that ever makes me  feel small but we send you know 90  billion messages a month across these  channels and you know regularly exceed  500 thousand messages a second and our  customer base we're pretty much geared  for enterprise all the way of customers  large and small and we have you know 25%  of the fortune 100 our customers really  briefly this is a look at our platform  we talked a bit about channels we we  support many of them we also have this  open channel API that a number of our  customers use to reach on things like  over-the-top networks that they can't  reach through any other way and so  that's nice technology and we've been  investing heavily in our orchestration  layer so that's all sorts of things  targeting segmentation automation and  orchestration across channels optimizing  them and then last all these experiences  that we help our customers create send  back a lot of first party data to urban  airship and we help them make use of  that by providing rich user profiles  performance analytics on that data  machine learning and AI capabilities  things like predictive churn some time  optimization and then we have some  technology to stream any of this data  that we process out to our customers  other aspects of their marketing  technology stack things like BI tools  some of our customers used to send it to  ad networks that that sort of thing so a  couple examples of experiences that we  provide we work across verticals so  travel retail media financial I  mentioned a lot of Airlines our point of  view is the best marketing isn't really  marketing at all it's these useful types  of experiences where you can provide  value and experiences that users value  so you know in travel you know things  like hey your seat has been upgraded and  retail your package has shipped media a  story that you're interested in because  you're following a journalist financial  you know here's your your account  balance so we do all sorts of things  like that most recently just last night  I put this in there because it was  timely and the NCAA is a customer and so  we powered of their March Madness  messaging so they do a great job with  anything from squirrel Ertz to sending  videos through rich notifications to  even last night after the game you know  the kind of famous one shining moment  video was sent out so we we were busy  last night another quick story so so  last year for the World Cup soon after  we finished our migration to Google  cloud we had a bunch of customers that  were really excited about the world come  one in one in particular one football  really wanted to make sure that you know  their notifications arrived as quickly  as possible so we we worked closely with  them on some technology that we call  boost and this is just a graph this was  the end of the World Cup last year we  were sending you know four hundred  thousand notifications a second four  minutes that's like that's across our  customer base we power tons of media and  sports apps since then you know we've  exceeded this milestone and regularly  exceed five hundred note of five hundred  thousand notifications a second but just  to lift the hood up a bit  I won't delve into too much detail but  this is a look at one of our subsystems  we call it the CDP the core delivery  platform so it's you know the the scale  that we operate means that we we have to  do a lot of things that maybe we wish we  wouldn't have to in terms of  micro-services and really kind of  breaking some of these components apart  so we have what we call a distributed  query planner which deals with a lot of  these subsystems and it leverages a  bunch of google technology like BigTable  we leverage a ton of cloud pub/sub and  also compute engine but by leveraging  Google technology again compared back to  our Colo we were able to easily add  capacity for the World Cup we can scale  it up and down and we also were able to  leverage you know some of our own  innovation by doing a bunch of stop  software optimization to you know do a  lot of parallel processing some advanced  queueing of some of these priority  requests and so by doing that you know  we were able to really meet our  customers needs I think if we hadn't  completed our move to Google cloud just  because the World Cup was so so big for  our business you know it probably  wouldn't have gone nearly as well so  that was great so I mentioned that we we  started our journey 80% on Colo 20% on  Amazon we're expanding our footprint  more globally now so we're moving into  additional regions and so it just made  sense for a second  holiday we've had somewhat success with  Google our engineers really love it and  so we've just collapsed the stack this  is one of the projects that our team is  working on right now we're migrant we  have a business intelligence capability  so it's really fine-grained user level  data at the event level so just tons and  tons of data and it had been running on  it's currently running on redshift and  has been for a couple years and that's  worked okay but we're really our  customers love this product they want to  see better performance they want a  fresher data and so BigTable and  bigquery are really helping us deliver  on that for our customers and so  currently we're loading this data daily  just because it's so much and just given  some of the economics that at least we  experience with redshift but we're gonna  be able to move that to minutes instead  of per day so our customers has they've  been hearing about this news they're  pretty excited and also our storage is  now separated from compute if you know  anything about redshift this will make  sense to you so we can say goodbye to  nodes in reserved instances which is  great for our cost index you know just  from a complexity standpoint you know  some of this is just the way we built  the system we're doing a lot of software  updates to this as well but you know  with this new version of the system you  know we're moving from some complicated  ETL that we kind of crafted from Kinesis  and microservices to one one single  dataflow job so just really dramatically  simplifies the system and then just our  our maintenance overhead is reduced like  we don't have to deal with vacuums  anymore which has been less than awesome  and then bigquery cluster tables really  enable efficient queries versus and  we've had to do for some of the queries  given the complexity of the data have to  do a lot of scanning of the entire data  set which is really slow so that's great  so and then as we kind of look to the  future and we have big aspirations for  our company and creating more and more  capabilities and our customer engagement  platform  and so we're you know seeking to  leverage more and more of Google cloud  you know right now all of our machine  learning capabilities are built in-house  and we're experimenting with leveraging  some of the Google stuff you know our  job is to innovate further up the stack  and not bother with things like managing  HBase and hard drives so anyways that's  my story  thank you  [Music]
 

